  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Deriving the Convolution Integral",
    "lesson_core_move": "Derive the continuous-time convolution integral $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ by applying linearity and time invariance to the weighted shifted-impulse decomposition of an input signal.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Convolution Integral",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Deriving the Convolution Integral",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The object to recognize is the weighted shifted-impulse representation of the input. It says that the signal $x(t)$ can be built by superposing shifted impulses $\\delta(t - \\tau)$, where the weight on the impulse at location $\\tau$ is the signal value $x(\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To derive the output of an LTI system, handle one shifted impulse component at a time. Linearity lets us separate the components, and time invariance says that if $\\delta(t)$ produces $h(t)$, then a shifted impulse $\\delta(t - \\tau)$ produces the same shift in the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-\\tau)\\mapsto h(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The weight stays attached to that shifted response. So the component $x(\\tau)$$\\delta(t - \\tau)$ contributes $x(\\tau)$$h(t - \\tau)$ to the output; the weight multiplies the response, not the time argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\mapsto x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Finally, superpose all of the component responses over $\\tau$. That continuous superposition gives the convolution integral for the LTI system output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The core move is to replace each shifted impulse in the input decomposition with the matching shifted impulse response, keep the weight $x(\\tau)$, and then add every component response by integration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Convolution Integral",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Mapping a Shifted Impulse to a Shifted Impulse Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an LTI system with impulse response $h(t)$, determine the output produced by the input $\\delta(t-3)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is a shifted impulse with no extra weight. Since $h(t)$ is the response to the unshifted impulse $\\delta(t)$, the only rule needed is time invariance.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t)\\mapsto h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The impulse $\\delta(t - 3)$ is the unshifted impulse delayed by 3. For an LTI system, delaying the input by 3 delays the output by the same amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-3)\\mapsto h(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the output produced by the input $\\delta(t - 3)$ is the shifted impulse response $h(t - 3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=h(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Convolution Integral",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Applying the Input Weight to the Shifted Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an LTI system with impulse response $h(t)$, determine the output produced by the weighted shifted impulse $4\\delta(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is still one shifted impulse, but now it has a scalar weight. Separate the coefficient 4 from the unweighted shifted impulse $\\delta(t - 2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4\\delta(t-2)=4\\cdot\\delta(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First map the shifted impulse itself. Since $\\delta(t - 2)$ is delayed by 2, time invariance shifts the impulse response by the same amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-2)\\mapsto h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply homogeneity from linearity. Multiplying the input component by 4 multiplies the entire shifted response by 4; the coefficient does not change the time argument of h.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4\\delta(t-2)\\mapsto 4h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the output produced by the weighted shifted impulse is the shifted impulse response scaled by 4.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=4h(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Convolution Integral",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Superposing Responses from the Impulse Decomposition",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an LTI system with impulse response $h(t)$, write the output produced by the input $2\\delta(t-1)-\\delta(t-4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is now a finite sum of weighted shifted impulses. Use the same single-component rule on each term, then add the resulting responses by linearity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2\\delta(t-1)-\\delta(t-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the first component, $\\delta(t - 1)$ maps to $h(t - 1)$ by time invariance, and the coefficient 2 multiplies the entire shifted response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2\\delta(t-1)\\mapsto 2h(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the second component, $\\delta(t - 4)$ maps to $h(t - 4)$. The coefficient is -1, so the response term is negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-\\delta(t-4)\\mapsto -h(t-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Finally, superpose the component responses. This is the finite-sum version of the same add-the-responses idea that will become continuous superposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2h(t-1)-h(t-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Deriving the Convolution Integral",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Continuous-Time Convolution Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Starting from $x(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$, derive the expression for the output $y(t)$ of an LTI system with impulse response $h(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input is already written as a continuous superposition of weighted shifted impulses. Each value of $\\tau$ gives one small component to pass through the LTI system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Track one generic component. The shifted impulse is $\\delta(t - \\tau)$, so time invariance replaces it with the shifted impulse response $h(t - \\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t-\\tau)\\mapsto h(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The weight $x(\\tau)$ and the infinitesimal width d $\\tau$ stay attached to that component response. The weight multiplies the response; it does not change the time argument of h.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau\\mapsto x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now superpose all of those component responses over the same $\\tau$ range. Replacing the input integrand with the response integrand gives the LTI system output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the continuous-time convolution integral. The move here is to identify the output formula from the impulse decomposition, not to evaluate the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
